"'''The Twist'''" is an American song written and originally released in 1958 by [[Hank Ballard|Hank Ballard and the Midnighters]] as a [[A-side and B-side|B-side]] to "Teardrops on Your Letter".<ref>{{cite book |title=Top Pop Singles 1955-1999|last=Whitburn|first=Joel|author-link=Joel Whitburn|year=2000|publisher=Record Research, Inc.|location=Menomonee Falls, WI|isbn=0-89820-140-3|page=32}}</ref> It was inspired by the [[Twist (dance)|twist]] [[dance craze]]. Ballard's version was a moderate hit, peaking at number 28 on the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] in 1960.<ref>[http://www.billboard.com/bbcom/esearch/chart_display.jsp?cfi=379&cfgn=Singles&cfn=The+Billboard+Hot+100&ci=3069989&cdi=8780672&cid=07%2F18%2F1960 The Billboard Hot 100 Chart Listing For The Week Of Jul 18 1960], Billboard.com {{dead link|date=May 2013}}</ref> On the US ''Billboard'' [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs|Hot R&B Sides]] chart, the original version of "The Twist" first peaked at number 16 in 1959 and at number six in 1960.<ref>{{cite book |title=Top R&B/Hip-Hop Singles: 1942-2004|last=Whitburn |first=Joel |author-link=Joel Whitburn |year=2004 |publisher=Record Research |page=44}}</ref> By 1962, the record sold in excess of one million copies, becoming Ballard's fourth million seller.<ref name="The Book of Golden Discs">{{cite book |first=Joseph |last=Murrells |year=1978 |title=The Book of Golden Discs |edition=2nd |publisher=Barrie and Jenkins Ltd |location=London |page=121 |isbn=0-214-20512-6}}</ref> 

[[Chubby Checker]]'s 1960 [[cover version]] of the song reached number one on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 on September 19, 1960, where it stayed for one week and then went on to set a record at the time as the only song to reach number one during two different chart runs when it resurfaced and topped the Hot 100 again for two weeks starting on January 13, 1962.<ref name=pc20>{{Gilliland |url=https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc19770/m1/ |title=Show 20 – Forty Miles of Bad Road: Early '60s potpourri |show=20 |track=2}}</ref> This would not happen for another song for nearly 59 years until December 2020, when [[Mariah Carey]]'s "[[All I Want for Christmas Is You]]" reached the summit after previously topping in another separate chart run in December 2019.

On ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard's]]'' list of all-time No. 1 Hot 100 singles, which considers total weeks on the chart and chart position each week, "The Twist" held the top position from 2008 until September 2020, when "[[Blinding Lights]]" by [[the Weeknd]] took over the top position.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Watch the Throne|last=Mamo|first=Heran|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=November 20, 2021|volume=133|issue=16|page=5-}}</ref> In 2014, ''Billboard'' magazine declared the song the "biggest hit" of the 1960s.<ref name="BB2014" /> On Canada's [[CHUM Chart]]s, the songs were co-charted, reaching number two on August 22, 1960.<ref name="auto">{{cite web|url=https://chumtribute.com/60-08-22-chart.jpg |title=CHUM Hit Parade - August 22, 1960}}</ref>

